{
  "dom_id": "step-q47489",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The velocity of a motorcycle, measured in meters per second, is given by the function $v(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $v(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $10$ | $43$ | $70$ |\n\nApproximate the acceleration ${v}^{′}(t)$ when $t=9$ using a backward difference approximation.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{v}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{v(t)-v(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=9.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(9) & ≈\\frac{v(9)-v(9-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(9)-v(6)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{43-10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{3} \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is $11\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$",
